INT. COLLAPSED SUBWAY TUNNEL - NIGHT

Concrete, rebar, rubble. A single emergency light flickers in a cage on the wall. Dust hangs in the beam. Water drips somewhere out of sight.

MAGDA KOWAL (50s, transit worker, hard hat, reflective vest) leans against a slab, one hand on her ribs. RAVI MENON (20s, student, backpack, scuffed laptop) sits cross-legged, scrolling a dead phone. JOAQUIN BELTRAN (40s, commuter, suit jacket wrapped around a bleeding hand) paces. XIMENA ORTIZ (30s, pregnant, hand on her belly) rests on a plastic crate. OLD TEO (70s, flat cap, harmonica in his breast pocket) sits against the wall, eyes closed. BEA (9, school uniform, a pink backpack, sitting very close to Ximena) hums.

MAGDA
Everybody, listen. Rescue knows we're down here.

JOAQUIN
How?

MAGDA
Because they always know.

RAVI
(muttering)
Phone's dead. Mine's dead.

JOAQUIN
We all have dead phones, kid.

Ximena adjusts her seat. She winces and tries to hide it. Bea looks up at her and places a small hand over Ximena's.

BEA
Is the baby kicking?

XIMENA
A little.

BEA
Does it know we're in a tunnel?

XIMENA
I don't think so.

BEA
Good.

Old Teo opens one eye, takes the harmonica from his pocket, wipes it on his sleeve, and plays a single low note. It trembles in the cave.

MAGDA
Teo, save your breath.

TEO
It saves itself.

He plays another, then a phrase, slow and tender. Ravi stops scrolling. Joaquin stops pacing. Even Magda lowers her head. The light flickers. For the length of a verse, no one speaks.

JOAQUIN
(to himself)
My wife is going to kill me.

RAVI
It's not your fault.

JOAQUIN
I took the late train. I never take the late train.

BEA
I take it every day.

JOAQUIN
Do you?

BEA
My grandma meets me at Elm.

Ximena and Magda exchange a look over the child's head. Teo lowers the harmonica.

TEO
She'll be at Elm tonight too.

BEA
I know.

MAGDA
Okay. Okay. Water first. Joaquin, what have you got?

JOAQUIN
A bottle. Half.

MAGDA
Ravi?

RAVI
Nothing. A granola bar.

MAGDA
Break it in six.

RAVI
It's tiny.

MAGDA
Break it in six.

He does. He gives Bea the biggest piece. She hands it to Ximena. Ximena breaks it again and hands half back.

Overhead, concrete shifts with a groan. Dust sifts down in a thin ribbon. Everyone looks up. The light dims, brightens, holds.

TEO
(softly)
Play me a song, little one.

BEA
I don't know any.

TEO
Hum the one you were humming.

Bea hums. Teo finds the key on his harmonica and joins. The others sit and let the music do the work.
